{{Short description|Publishing format}}
{{Distinguish|Serial (publishing)}}
{{EngvarB|date=January 2022}}
[[File:Publicité pour Great Expectations dans All the Year Round.jpeg|thumb|right|Advertisement for [[Charles Dickens]]' ''[[Great Expectations]]'', serialised weekly ("to be continued") in the British literary magazine ''[[All the Year Round]]'' from December 1860 to August 1861]]
{{Literature}}

In [[literature]], a '''serial''' is a printing or publishing format by which a single larger [[Creative work|work]], often a work of [[Narrative|narrative fiction]], is published in smaller, sequential installments. The installments are also known as ''numbers'', ''parts'', '''''fascicules''''' or '''''fascicles'''''<!--boldface per WP:R#PLA; 'Fascicle (book)' redirects here-->, and may be released either as separate [[publication]]s or within sequential issues of a [[periodical publication]], such as a magazine or newspaper.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Law |first=Graham |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=qVrUTUelE6YC |title=Dictionary of Nineteenth-Century Journalism |publisher=Academia Press |year=2009 |isbn=9789038213408 |editor-last=Brake |editor-first=Laurel |location=London |page=567 |chapter=Serials and the Nineteenth-Century Publishing Industry |editor-last2=Demoor |editor-first2=Marysa |chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=qVrUTUelE6YC&pg=PA567}}</ref>

Serialisation can also begin with a single short story that is subsequently turned into a series.  Historically, such series have been published in periodicals. Popular short-story series are often published together in book form as collections.

