[[File:Tétraévangéliaire_bohaïrique_-_BNF_Copte_13_f6v_-_Baptême_du_Christ.jpg|thumb|[[Miniature (illuminated manuscript)|Miniature]] depicting the [[baptism of Christ]] from a late 12th-century [[Illuminated manuscript|illuminated]] copy of the [[Gospels]]]]
'''Coptic literature''' is the body of writings in the [[Coptic language]] of [[Egypt]], the last stage of the indigenous [[Egyptian language]]. It is written in the [[Coptic alphabet]]. The study of the Coptic language and literature is called [[Coptology]].

{{Short description|Area of scientific research}}
{{about|the early modern usage of the term|the modern treatment of chemistry of gases|Gas-phase ion chemistry}}
[[Image:Boyle air pump.jpg|150px|thumb|right|[[Robert Boyle]]'s air pump]]

In the [[history of science]], '''pneumatic chemistry''' is an area of [[scientific research]] of the seventeenth, eighteenth, and early nineteenth centuries. Important goals of this work were the understanding of the physical properties of [[gas]]es and how they relate to [[chemical reaction]]s and, ultimately, the composition of [[matter]]. The rise of [[phlogiston theory]], and its replacement by a new theory after the discovery of [[oxygen]] as a gaseous component of the [[Earth atmosphere]] and a [[chemical reagent]] participating in the [[combustion]] reactions, were addressed in the era of pneumatic chemistry.

